<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kru people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kru are a West African people of the coast of south-eastern Liberia and south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Côte d'Ivoire</a>, speaking Kru languages. From the 18th century they became renowned as sailors and dock workers, serving on European ships along the West African coast and settling in ports as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freetown">Freetown</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a>. In Liberia they were among the indigenous peoples long excluded from power by the Americo-Liberian settler elite.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kru-people.jpg" alt="Kru people" width="320" height="352" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public Domain · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piroguiers_Kroumen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Liberian society was long divided between the Americo-Liberian settlers and their descendants, a few per cent of the population concentrated in coastal towns, and the indigenous majority, including the Kpelle, Bassa, Grebo, <b>Kru</b>, Vai, Gola, Kissi, Loma, Mano, Dan and Mandinka.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liberia">Liberia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Settlers farmed and traded with the <b>Kru people</b> of the coast, but disease, shortages and conflict with local communities made life hard, and the colony depended on help from other settlements.</blockquote><a href="/state/mississippi-in-africa">Mississippi-in-Africa</a></li></ul></section>
